# Line-ending and diff rules. `core.autocrlf` is true on the maintainer's machines, so text is
# stored LF and checked out CRLF; these are the files where that is wrong or where the default
# guess is unsafe.

# Tensor fixtures: little-endian float32, no header, read by offset.
#
# Marked binary explicitly rather than left to git's content heuristic, because the heuristic is
# wrong here in a way that only shows up later. Git decides text-or-binary by looking for a NUL
# byte in the FIRST 8000 BYTES; `mel-silence.f32` is 48 KB of a repeating non-zero word followed by
# the zero padding, so its first NUL is at byte 40960 and the sniff never reaches it. That file
# would be classified as text, have every 0x0A in it rewritten to 0x0D 0x0A on checkout, and arrive
# at the test suite as a different tensor — on a fresh clone, on someone else's machine, with a
# failure that looks like a broken port rather than a broken checkout.
*.f32 binary

# Written by scripts/make-diariser-fixtures.py and scripts/validate-der.py, both of which emit LF
# and both of which compare what they would write against what is on disk. Checked out as CRLF,
# every re-run reports every fixture as changed and the "re-running must be a no-op" guarantee
# those scripts are built around stops meaning anything.
tests/fixtures/diarisation/**/expected.json text eol=lf

# The diariser's parity reference: a NumPy .npy array of float32 probabilities, compared against
# this machine's own to decide whether a GPU backend reproduces the CPU. An .npy begins with a magic
# string and a version pair that puts a NUL inside the first ten bytes, so git's sniff gets this one
# right unaided — marked anyway, on the same grounds as the .f32 rule above. A fixture whose 0x0A
# bytes were rewritten on checkout would not fail loudly; it would fail as a parity check that
# rejects every backend on somebody else's clone, including the CPU.
*.npy binary

# The translator's parity fixture and the sentences it is taken over. Written by the sidecar's own
# `writeParityReference` op, which writes LF explicitly, and the sources by a generator that does the
# same. Under the default CRLF conversion every regeneration would report both files as changed, and
# the "re-running must be a no-op" property they are checked by would stop meaning anything — the
# same rule, for the same reason, as the diarisation fixtures above. (The diariser's own reference is
# an `.npy` and is covered by the binary rule above.)
python/uindosill_engines/translator/parity-reference.json text eol=lf
python/uindosill_engines/translator/parity-sources.json text eol=lf

# Vendored NVIDIA NeMo source, kept LF so that a checkout is byte-identical to upstream rather than
# merely equivalent to it. That is not tidiness: NOTICE.md claims these two files are unmodified
# under Apache-2.0 §4(b), and the way anybody checks such a claim is to diff a checkout against
# NVIDIA's. Under the default CRLF conversion every line of both files would differ, and the
# claim would look false to exactly the person taking the trouble to verify it.
python/uindosill_engines/_vendor/** text eol=lf

# The two embedded typefaces. TrueType begins with a version tag full of NUL bytes, so git's
# text-or-binary sniff gets these right on its own — but the rule above exists because that sniff
# is a heuristic, and a font checked out with its 0x0A bytes rewritten is a font that fails to
# load on somebody else's clone with no obvious cause. Cheaper to say so than to diagnose.
*.ttf binary
